  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAs the boundary layer continues to moisten with of a frontal\nwave, I expect that cloud cover as well as marine fog and\nstratus will continue to expand thru today. The thing to watch\nwill be how quickly marine fog and stratus can expand inland.\nHi-res guidance is currently forecast this to occur late\nafternoon to early evening.\n\nOtherwise attention is mostly on the first rainfall chance as\nthat wave propagates thru southern New England. The sharp\nnorthern edge of rainfall will be in the forecast area\nsomewhere, but south of that some beneficial rainfall amounts\nlook likely. There has been a steady ramp up in ensemble QPF\namounts for the last couple of days, especially across southern\nNH. Here forecast soundings indicate some instability and\nconvective rainfall rates are possible. CAM guidance does show\nsome potential for amounts up to 1 inch or so. We will\ncertainly take it. In the central part of the forecast area,\nlike IZG, prospects are more likely 50/50 on getting much in the\nway of rain. Winds turn northwest and will clear out the rain\nand any fog, see Key Message 2 Description for more information\nabout winds on Fri.\n\nAs we head into the weekend the next rain event looms on Sun. A\nstout high pressure will be retreating slightly from a position\nnorth of the forecast area. Model guidance wants to lift another\nfrontal wave into the area and bring an area of rain with it.\nEnsemble guidance continues to waffle a bit on placement of this\nrain leaving it a lower confidence forecast. Given that forcing\nis weakening as it cross the local area I do like the NBM PoP\ntrending down as it moves east. However it is also showing\nlikely PoP at this range which may be a little high for my\ntastes right now. But this could very easily turn into another\nhigh PoP/low QPF events that have been fairly common from the\nmid to latter half of the warm season.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper level low will drop south out of Quebec on Friday, \nsending a surface cold front across the region. A tightening \npressure gradient will allow for gusty northwesterly winds with \ngusts between 30-35 mph likely. Other than some upslope clouds \nand perhaps a shower, it should be mainly sunny with high \ntemperatures ranging from the 60s across the north with 70s to \nthe south. Friday night will be chilly with low temperatures \nranging from the lower to middle 30s across interior valleys \nwith lower 40s elsewhere. Patchy frost is possible, especially \nacross the sheltered valleys. \n\nMean trofing will prevail across New England Saturday through \nmuch of next week with negative H5 height anomalies. This will \nkeep temperatures generally below average with daytime highs \ninto the lower to middle 60s most days with overnight lows into \nthe 30s and 40s. Will need to watch each night closely as we get\ncloser in time for possible frost/freeze conditions.",
